<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Matching Correlation Coefficients</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00761</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Matching Correlation Coefficients</md:title>
    <md:uuid>73e50bc3-097d-4808-8152-6eceaf47a87e</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Thinking back to the last activity, you learned about the <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>r</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>-value. Write these characteristics
        about the <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>r</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>-value.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">The <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>r</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>-value is called a correlation
        coefficient. A <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">correlation coefficient</term>
        is one way to measure the strength of a linear relationship. </para>
      <list id="list-00001">
        <item>The sign of <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>r</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> is the same as the sign of the
          slope of the best fit line.</item>
        <item>The values for <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>r</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> go from –1 to 1 and include –1
          and 1.</item>
        <item>The closer <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>r</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> is to 1 or –1, the stronger the
          linear relationship between the variables.</item>
        <item>The closer <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>r</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> is to 0, the weaker the linear
          relationship between the variables.</item>
      </list>
      <para id="para-00003">Take turns with your partner to match a scatter plot with a correlation
        coefficient. For each match you find, explain to your partner how you know it’s a match. For
        each match your partner finds, listen carefully to their explanation. If you disagree,
        discuss your thinking and work to reach an agreement.</para>
      <para id="para-00004">A</para>
      <media alt="A scatter plot with black dots shows data points forming a straight line trend. A blue line passes through the points, indicating a positive linear relationship between x (0–10) and y (2–9) on labeled axes.">
        <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_06_09_01.svg"/>
      </media>
      <para id="para-00005">B</para>
      <media alt="Scatter plot with black dots showing data points trending upward, a blue line representing the line of best fit, x-axis labeled from 0 to 90, and y-axis labeled from 0 to 500.">
        <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_06_09_02.svg"/>
      </media>
      <para id="para-00006">C</para>
      <media alt="A scatter plot with black dots shows a negative correlation between x and y values. A blue trendline slopes downward from left to right, indicating a decreasing linear relationship. The axes are labeled x and y.">
        <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_06_09_03.svg"/>
      </media>
      <para id="para-00007">D</para>
      <media alt="A scatter plot with black dots showing data points and a blue trend line sloping downward from left to right, indicating a negative correlation between the x and y variables.">
        <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_06_09_04.svg"/>
      </media>
      <para id="para-00008">E</para>
      <media alt="A scatter plot with black dots showing data points, a blue trend line indicating a positive correlation, and axes labeled x (horizontal) and y (vertical), with x ranging from 0 to 10 and y from 0 to 10.">
        <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_06_09_05.svg"/>
      </media>
      <para id="para-00009">F</para>
      <media alt="Scatter plot with black data points and a blue line showing a negative linear relationship between x (0 to 10) and y (8 to 2). As x increases, y decreases. Axes are labeled x and y.">
        <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_06_09_06.svg"/>
      </media>
      <para id="para-00010">G</para>
      <media alt="A scatter plot with black dots showing data points, a light blue line representing the line of best fit, and labeled axes: x (horizontal) and y (vertical). The trend line has a slight negative slope.">
        <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_06_09_07.svg"/>
      </media>
      <para id="para-00011">H</para>
      <media alt="A scatter plot with black dots showing data points and a blue trend line sloping upward, indicating a positive correlation between x and y values. Axes are labeled x and y.">
        <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_06_09_08.svg"/>
      </media>

